</span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Shanghai Jiao Tong University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">ziho_he@sjtu.edu.cn   wylin@sjtu.edu.cn </span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the prosperity of the video language model, the current pursuit of comprehensive video reasoning is thwarted by the inherent spatio-temporal incompleteness within individual videos, resulting in hallucinations and inaccuracies.
A promising solution is to augment the reasoning performance with multiple related videos. However, video tokens are numerous and contain redundant information, so directly feeding the relevant video data into a large language model to enhance responses could be counterproductive.
To address this challenge, we propose a multi-video collaborative framework for video language models. For efficient and flexible video representation, we establish a Video Structuring Module to represent the video’s knowledge as a spatio-temporal graph. Based on the structured video representation, we design the Graph Fusion Module to fuse the structured knowledge and valuable information from related videos into the augmented graph node tokens. Finally, we construct an elaborate multi-video structured prompt to integrate the graph, visual, and textual tokens as the input to the large language model. Extensive experiments substantiate the effectiveness of our framework, showcasing its potential as a promising avenue for advancing video language models.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13161v1/head_1.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/730;" width="685" height="730" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S0.F1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Video question-answering pipeline under different video collaboration strategies. (a) <span id="S0.F1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single-video reasoning pipeline</span>; (b) <span id="S0.F1.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct multi-video collaboration pipeline:</span> concatenate multiple video’s visual tokens, which is burdensome; (c) <span id="S0.F1.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structured multi-video collaboration pipeline</span> (ours).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S0.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13161v1/head_single_vs_multiple.png" id="S0.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/213;" width="685" height="213" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S0.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Video question answering examples from video language models.<span id="S0.F2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span id="S0.F2.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Single-video reasoning (Left):</span> In video-1, the environmental visual cues are hard for the model to perceive, leading to a ’sports team’ hallucination based on only the textual query and linguistic priors. In Video 2, ice-related visuals are missing, and limited bartending knowledge causes the model to skip the question. <span id="S0.F2.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-video reasoning (Right):</span> Introducing relevant videos allows the model to complete and summarize domain-specific knowledge (such as environmental protection or bartending in this case), leading to more reliable and accurate answers.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The significant advancement in Large Language Models(LLMs) gives rise to the prosperity of video language models. Equipped with LLMs’ strong general knowledge, video language models showcase an impressive potential to comprehend complex video contents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
These models effectively bridge the semantic chasm between video and language, facilitating a nuanced integration of visual perception with language processing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, it remains an elusive goal to achieve reliable and comprehensive video reasoning. Specifically, the spatio-temporal knowledge captured from an individual video is often incomplete due to the limited <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">visual perception</span> and <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">comprehending ability</span>. Factors hindering visual perception include sparse sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, spatial occlusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and perspective switching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>). The comprehending ability is mainly constrained by the complexity of video content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and limited effective context length <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Since video language models mostly originate from large language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> with the help of visual instruction tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, video language models can only resort to the language prior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> when faced with knowledge incompleteness. This tendency brings spurious hallucination and misalignment with the visual facts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> to the answers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The mainstream of video language models follows a single-video reasoning pipeline (shown in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a)). Motivated by the research in multi-data collaboration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> and retrieval-augmented generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, a promising idea to address this limitation is integrating knowledge from multiple related videos. We illustrate some video question-answering examples in Figure <a href="#S0.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. As the examples demonstrate, single-video reasoning suffers from incomplete and redundant information from an individual video. These challenges prevent video language models from grasping valuable knowledge and lead to hallucinations and failures in answering the given questions. Introducing multiple highly related videos within the same domain can compensate for the missing information and enable the video language models to conclude and provide reliable answers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the apparent advantages, the incremental information from multiple videos does not necessarily lead to better reasoning in real cases. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b), A direct multi-video joint strategy is to concatenate the visual features of multiple videos in the prompt before passing them to the video language models, which can bring an overwhelming number of tokens to video language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. When exposed to long context, empirical evidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> proves that LLMs are inclined to concentrate only on specific and limited segments of the input, neglecting potentially critical information. Moreover, the high dimensionality, redundancy, and obfuscation of video content contrast sharply with the structured, clear, and rule-based nature of language. This temporal visual complexity makes the synthesis and refinement of multi-video knowledge particularly challenging.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present a structured multi-video collaborative reasoning framework. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c), Our framework achieves the alignment and completion of multi-video information at the graph-structured representation level.
Under this framework, to address the redundancy and complexity of video information, we first design the Video Structuring Module (VSM). This module leverages detection and tracking to extract key targets and their spatio-temporal relationships, thereby constructing a data-efficient structured video representation. After obtaining the structured representation, we propose the Graph Fusion Module (GFM) to yield LLM-friendly graph tokens. GFM integrates the structural information into node features through the graph attention network (GAT) and then fuses the multi-video knowledge based on cross-graph attention. Finally, we arrange the fused graph tokens, target video tokens, and text tokens with an elaborately designed prompt to augment VLMs with the multi-video structured knowledge. Overall, our contributions are three-fold:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explore a feasible structured reasoning framework for multi-video collaboration on video language models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under this framework, we develop the Video Structuring Module to obtain the data-efficient spatio-temporal graph for video information. Then, we further design the Graph Fusion Module to fuse the spatio-temporal relations and cross-video knowledge into the graph tokens.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments show that our framework can boost the reliability and accuracy through multi-video structuring and collaboration.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Vision Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the field of visual understanding, significant advancements have been achieved along with the development of large language models. By learning joint the representation of vision and language through feature alignment and visual instruction tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, vision-language models leverage the generalist capabilities that emerge from the large-scale training of large language models, enabling understanding of images and videos in open-world settings. Among them, Video-LLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> and BLIP-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> adopt the Q-former to extract valuable visual information into compact and LLM-friendly visual tokens for alignment. On the contrary, Flamingo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and BLIP-3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> utilize the scalable perceiver resampler to obtain learnable visual tokens. LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, Video-LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, and Qwen2-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> use an MLP-based projector to map the visual and textual inputs into a shared feature space. Besides, due to the complicated information contained in image and video input, many studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> aim to reduce the number of visual tokens for alleviating the computational and understanding burden on LLMs. While current methods focus on single-video reasoning, we try to explore a structured video reasoning framework to effectively address the challenges of redundancy and integration brought by multi-video inputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Multi-Data Collaboration</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although most deep learning task follows a single data processing pipeline, multi-data collaboration provides a promising direction to improve performances with the inner correspondence among multiple samples. We can roughly divide current works into two streams, which are content-related and task-related collaborations. Content-related collaboration helps models to focus on vital content through comparison among several related data. For example, co-segmentation task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> explores ways to segment the same object in different scenes by concluding and sharing the same object-related features. Few-shot image classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, action recognition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and fine-grained classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> methods focus on the key differences for accurate classification through multi-data comparison. Retrieval-Augmented Generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> is also a promising content-related collaboration that provides the LLMs with supporting information from retrieved related data. In task-related collaboration, models learn the way to complete the task by observing multiple samples performing the same task. For example, multi-video summarization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> researches aim to generate a summary from a collection of videos through multi-data complementary and refinement. In-Context Learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> uses examples with task guidance and answering to demonstrate to LLMs how to fulfill the tasks.
For video language models, current multi-video collaboration strategy directly concatenates multiple inputs, leading to burdensome redundancy and difficulties in collaboration. Building on these advancements, we introduce the multi-video reasoning task and achieve the compensation and refinement of key spatio-temporal information through multi-video collaboration.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13161v1/framework.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:617/396;" width="617" height="396" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Multi-video collaborative reasoning framework.<span id="S2.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Together with the target video, <math id="S2.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> related videos are retrieved to facilitate the reasoning process. First, we design the Video Structuring Module to obtain the structured video representation. Then, the Graph Fusion Module fuses the structure information and related videos’ information to get the video graph tokens. Finally, according to designed prompts, the graph tokens, visual tokens and text tokens are arranged as input to the large language model for question answering.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Overview</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>The setting of multi-video reasoning</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we explore the potential of utilizing multi-video information for video understanding. Specifically, we follow a multi-video setting, where a target video <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is accompanied by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> related videos <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V_{1},V_{2},\dots,V_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V_{1},V_{2},\dots,V_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. To retrieve related videos, their feature vectors are constructed in advance for efficient video retrieval. Different ways of video vectorization are discussed in supplementary materials. Finally, the methods are required to answer the question to the target video with the help of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> retrieved videos.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Framework overview</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our multi-video collaborative reasoning framework is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 Multi-Data Collaboration ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
First, the Video Structuring Module is introduced in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Video Structuring Module ‣ 3 Method ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> to get the structured video representation.
Based on the obtained video structure, we design the Graph Fusion Module in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Graph Fusion Module ‣ 3 Method ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> to fuse the structure information and select useful related videos’ information to the resulting graph tokens.
Finally, we arrange all graph, visual, and text tokens according to our designed multi-video reasoning prompt and send them to the large language model.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Video Structuring Module</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Efficient structured video representation paves the way for the following integration of multi-video knowledge. Given the video and its paired dense caption, the process of our Video Structuring Module (VSM) is depicted on the right bottom of Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 Multi-Data Collaboration ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which can be specified as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: Scene detection.</span> To avoid the video’s temporal redundancy, we use a light-weighted content-based scene detector <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PySceneDetect</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> to detect scenes from the video. Then, the middle frame of each scene is extracted as the keyframes for the later video structuring process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: Dense video captioning.</span> To prepare for the latter structuring pipeline, we need to extract the detailed and fine-grained textual concept. To achieve this goal, we adopt a video language model to give a detailed description of the input video through prompt design, which is detailed in our supplementary materials.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3: Textual scene graph parsing.</span> We then extract the textual scene graph <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{\text{Text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>Text</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{\text{Text}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the dense video caption with the help of the <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SceneGraphParser</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. The textual scene graph is composed of several triplets <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}=\{s_{i},p_{i},o_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}=\{s_{i},p_{i},o_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th triplets and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i},p_{i},o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i},p_{i},o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the subject, predicate and object of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Triplets are fundamental objects of interaction and event within the video, formatted in ⟨subject - predicate - object⟩.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 4: Graph-guided grounding.</span> Based on the textual scene graph <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{\text{Text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>Text</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{\text{Text}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can detect the interacted objects using the open-set detector <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GroundingDINO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Then, we use the tracking method <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DEVA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> to establish the tracklets of the former grounded objects, which temporally connect the same objects. Through detecting and tracking, we can get the: 1) grounding results <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{B}=\{(b_{s_{i}},b_{o_{i}})|1\leq i\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=\{(b_{s_{i}},b_{o_{i}})|1\leq i\leq M</annotation></semantics></math>}, where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{s_{i}},b_{o_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{s_{i}},b_{o_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the bounding boxes of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and 2) tracking results <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{\text{Tid}_{s_{i}},\text{Tid}_{o_{i}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mtext>Tid</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>Tid</mtext><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{\text{Tid}_{s_{i}},\text{Tid}_{o_{i}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Tid}_{s_{i}},\text{Tid}_{o_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Tid</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>Tid</mtext><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Tid}_{s_{i}},\text{Tid}_{o_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the tracking ID of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Notice that triplets without grounded objects are redundant and should be filtered.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 5: Video graph establishment.</span> Built upon <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{\text{Text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext>Text</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{\text{Text}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we establish the graph representation for both the target video and related videos. Our graph consists of the <span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">nodes</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intra-frame edges</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inter-frame edges</span>. The <span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">node</span> represents the features of the object in the video. To get the node-level features, we use the detecting results <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> to crop them from the keyframes and use OpenCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> to extract their visual features. Specifically, we only utilize the [CLS] token as the output. Since the regions are pre-cropped, the [CLS] token already retains a fine-grained feature representation, eliminating the need to use all output tokens. This significantly reduces the number of tokens required to represent the visual modality. The <span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intra-frame edge</span> is represented by the predicates of each triplet, which indicates the spatial and interacting relationships between objects within one frame. The <span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inter-frame edge</span> connects objects in different frames with the same tracking ID, indicating their temporal relationships. Through the above steps, we can establish the graph-structured representation of both the target video and its related videos for further collaboration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Graph Fusion Module</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Graph Fusion Module (GFM) is a multi-layer stacked architecture (see Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 Multi-Data Collaboration ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), with each layer comprising two key components: the Hierarchical Frame Graph Attention Network (HF-GAT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> and the Cross-Graph Attention (CGA) mechanism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">HF-GAT is tailored to fuse graph-structured data within a single video. Traditional Graph Attention Networks (GATs) are primarily used for tasks such as node classification and relation prediction, where relationships between nodes are explicitly defined. In contrast, for both inter-frame and intra-frame contexts, these relationships are often implicit or absent. To address this challenge, as introduced in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Video Structuring Module ‣ 3 Method ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we propose converting the original visual modality data into a graph-structured representation. In this graph, nodes represent features of subjects or objects, which are extracted using OpenCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. For intra-frame edges, we utilize relationships based on triplets, where a directional link is formed from <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. For inter-frame edges, we leverage the results from the tracker <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to link <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> from the previous frame to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in the current frame. Additionally, we introduce bidirectional links for inter-frame connections, as it enhances the system’s ability to comprehend video content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Once the structured features from individual videos are extracted using HF-GAT, the next step is to select the most relevant information from related videos for the target video. To accomplish this, we introduce the cross-graph attention mechanism. Specifically, we use the target video <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{tar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{tar}</annotation></semantics></math> as the query <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{tar}=\mathbf{X}_{tar}\mathbf{W}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{tar}=\mathbf{X}_{tar}\mathbf{W}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the concatenation of the target video and related videos along the frame dimension serves as the key and value, i.e. <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{V}_{tar},\mathbf{V}_{rel}]=[\mathbf{X}_{tar},\mathbf{X}_{rel}]\mathbf{W}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{V}_{tar},\mathbf{V}_{rel}]=[\mathbf{X}_{tar},\mathbf{X}_{rel}]\mathbf{W}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{K}_{tar},\mathbf{K}_{rel}]=[\mathbf{X}_{tar},\mathbf{X}_{rel}]\mathbf{W}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{K}_{tar},\mathbf{K}_{rel}]=[\mathbf{X}_{tar},\mathbf{X}_{rel}]\mathbf{W}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[,]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[,]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes concatenation operation along the frame dimension. The concatenation order of related videos is unordered and interchangeable, so we do not explicitly introduce positional encoding, drawing inspiration from the Set Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. The positional encoding within individual videos is implicitly provided by the structured information embedded in the data, as the input to the cross-graph attention mechanism is derived from the output of HF-GAT.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we use class embedding (CE) to enable the cross-graph attention mechanism to distinguish between the graph of the target video and the graphs of related videos.</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{CE}_{tar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>CE</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{CE}_{tar}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma(\boldsymbol{\alpha})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜶</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma(\boldsymbol{\alpha}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{CE}_{rel}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>CE</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{CE}_{rel}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1-\sigma(\boldsymbol{\alpha})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜶</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1-\sigma(\boldsymbol{\alpha}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a learnable parameter shared across frames.
We then apply CE directly to the query, key, and value by adding it to each component:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E2X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\hat{Q}}_{tar}=\mathbf{Q}_{tar}+\mathrm{CE}_{tar}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>CE</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\hat{Q}}_{tar}=\mathbf{Q}_{tar}+\mathrm{CE}_{tar},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E2Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\hat{K}}_{rel}=\mathbf{K}_{rel}+\mathrm{CE}_{rel},\quad\mathbf{\hat{V}}_{rel}=\mathbf{V}_{rel}+\mathrm{CE}_{rel}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐊</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>CE</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>CE</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\hat{K}}_{rel}=\mathbf{K}_{rel}+\mathrm{CE}_{rel},\quad\mathbf{\hat{V}}_{rel}=\mathbf{V}_{rel}+\mathrm{CE}_{rel}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13161v1/prompts.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/596;" width="685" height="596"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Structured multi-video prompts.<span id="S3.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We properly integrate the multi-modal tokens, together with the one-shot and Chain-of-Thought guidances, to form an LLM-friendly input.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By leveraging CE, the cross-graph attention mechanism implicitly learns to distinguish between the target video and the related videos. The final output of the cross-graph attention is produced by concatenating <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{O}_{tar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐎</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{O}_{tar}</annotation></semantics></math>, the fused representation of the target video, with <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{rel}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{rel}</annotation></semantics></math>, the original representation of the related videos that are initially fed into the attention mechanism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We apply residual connections and pre-norm for both the HF-GAT and cross-graph attention components. Specifically, we use RMSNorm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, which is commonly employed in large language models (LLMs), for pre-norm. Notably, we omit the Feed-Forward Network (FFN) in this layer to maintain the alignment of features without excessive shifting.
The graph-structured representations of the videos are then processed through the GFM to construct graph tokens, each corresponding to the features of subjects or objects after being fused with the structured information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Structured Multi-Video Prompt</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Upon obtaining the fused multi-video graph tokens, we need to integrate the graph, video, and text tokens together to create an LLM-friendly prompt. Therefore, we propose the structured multi-video prompt, as is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3 Graph Fusion Module ‣ 3 Method ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Our prompt originates from previous video language models’ prompt design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. For target video, we maintain the video tokens
<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">VIDEO_TOKENS<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></span> of the target video to preserve fine-grained and detailed visual information. We also append its graph-structured data <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">GRAPH_TOKENS<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></span> to indicate the key object and spatio-temporal relationships. For <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> related videos, we only maintain the concise and data-efficient graph-structured data <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">GRAPH_TOKENS<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></span>. In this context, we further indicate the relationships between the target video and related videos and how the LLMs can utilize these related multi-video structured data. Moreover, we introduce Chain-of-Thought (CoT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> reasoning through the fixed prompt “Think through the process step by step”. This approach helps the model to sequentially reason through structured information, improving its ability to comprehend and process the graph-based features.
By structuring the prompt in this manner, we enable the video language model to leverage multi-video information in an effective way, thereby enhancing the model’s ability to reason about and answer queries related to the video content.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experiment Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset descriptions.</span>
At the training stage, we finetune our model on a subset of VideoInstruct-100K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> dataset with 10K video-text instructions together with the structured video graph. For evaluation, we test our method on three Video-QA datasets, including two public open-ended video question-answering datasets: ActivityNet-QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> and MSRVTT-QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. Besides, we also establish a high-quality domain-related Video-QA dataset named <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">InternVid-QA</span>. We meticulously selected 631 videos across 41 major domains from the large-scale video dataset InternVid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. We illustrated the statistics and some video examples in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1 Experiment Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. InternVid-QA’s videos cover various domains of life (see Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1 Experiment Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(a)) and are annotated with high-quality, domain-relevant question-answer pairs (see Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1 Experiment Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(b)). InternVid-QA’s setting aligns more closely with the objectives of our multi-video collaborative understanding. InternVid-QA will be public available for research use.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation details.</span>
Our proposed structured multi-video collaboration framework is adaptive to general video language models. To conduct experiments, we uses Vicuna-7B v1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> as the language model and LanguageBind <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> based on ViT-L/14 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> as its video encoder, following Video-LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.
For the Graph Fusion Module, the output node-level feature’s dimension is 1024. Since Video-LLaVA has pre-trained the projection layer to align the OpenCLIP’s visual features with LLM’s text tokens, we just pass the 1024-dim node feature to the projector and get the 4096-dim graph tokens.
We initialize our video language model with Video-LLaVA 7B’s model weights and empower the model with the capability to learn from multi-video graphs through finetuning. In the fine-tuning process, the vision and text encoder are frozen. For the remaining network, we use the AdamW optimizer with a cosine learning rate schedule, whose initial learning rate is <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> and the warmup ratio is 0.03. We train our model on 8 A6000 GPUs with a total batch size of 32.
For each video, we uniformly sample 8 frames and resize each frame to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224\times 224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>224</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224\times 224</annotation></semantics></math>. Each video is accompanied by 5 related videos during training and experiments are conducted on InternVid-QA without special comments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13161v1/dataset.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/655;" width="685" height="655" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">The InternVid-QA dataset.<span id="S4.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (a) Statistic results of video number distribution over 41 different domains. (b) Illustration of some video examples with question and answer labels on the “painting” and “salad” domains.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Video question answering performances on different large video-language models.<span id="S4.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Following Video-ChatGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, we employ ChatGPT-Assistant (gpt-3.5-turbo) to report the accuracy and score of the open-set answering.</span></span></figcaption>
