</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Ablation study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation study on the proposed components.</span>
Our framework mainly consists of two parts, which are video structuring and multi-video collaboration. We conduct ablation study on our proposed components to validate their effectiveness. Experimental results are shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Zero-shot Video Question Answering ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We first disable video structuring module to test different common multi-video fusion strategies. Here “multi-video tokens” means concatenate all videos’ tokens as the input to context; “multi-video captions” means sending all videos’ captions to context. By observation, “multi-video tokens” strategy introduces overwhelming tokens to the context and brings excessive understanding challenges, yielding bad performance. “multi-video captions” can slightly improve the performance (1.2%) with the help of related videos. Then, we enable the Video Structuring Module and find that a structured and clear video representation is beneficial to LLM’s understanding, improving the performance by 0.5%. Finally, by adopting multi-video graph fusion, the framework can effectively extract useful information from related videos and get 3.4% additional performance improvement, at the cost of only 0.2K overhead of token numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation on Graph Fusion Module design.</span>
We further conduct an ablation study on the design of our Graph Fusion Module. Our GFM is composed of two components: HG-GAT and CGA. Since we have discussed the usage of FFN in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Graph Fusion Module ‣ 3 Method ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, we also take it into consideration and conduct ablation study on these three components. Ablation results are shown in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Zero-shot Video Question Answering ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In the first row, the graph structure feature tokens are directly sent to the multimodal projection layer to get the graph tokens. Then in the second row, we adopt HF-GAT to convey structure information to each token, increasing the performance by 1.6%. Then, with the help of CGA, multi-video knowledge is fused into graph tokens and further improves the performance by 1.7%. The adoption of FFN doesn’t bring improvement to the reasoning accuracy, which further corroborates the conclusion in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Graph Fusion Module ‣ 3 Method ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> above.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13161v1/video_num.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:617/363;" width="617" height="363"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Accuracy under different video numbers.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13161v1/visualization.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:651/340;" width="651" height="340" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization of our structured multi-video collaborative reasoning.<span id="S4.F7.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We visualize a representative video question-answering example in our structured multi-video collaboration pipeline, including the grounding and structuring results, the cross-graph attention map, as well as the answers before and after adopting our framework.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13161v1/video_relevance.svg" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:617/359;" width="617" height="359"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S4.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Accuracy under different video relevance.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Discussion on the retrieved video contents</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How do multiple videos affect the performance?</span> Multi-video data can help to achieve more comprehensive reasoning, In this section, we discuss the effect of the number of the related videos. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.3 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, when the retrieval number increased from 0 to 7, we can observe that the accuracy first rises then drops, which peaks at 5.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How does video relevance affect the performance?</span> In the main experiment, we arrange most related videos to the target video within each iteration. In this part, we rearrange the retrieved videos with different lower similarities (cosine similarity between video features) to test how video relevance affects the performance. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.3 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, the reasoning performance decreases as the relevance of the video diminishes, while the performance is still comparable to our competitors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">conclusion.</span> More relevant videos can lead to better performance, but the key is to find videos containing critical information. Introducing more or unrelated videos will unavoidably introduce irrelevant information, yet our method possesses the ability to filter out irrelevant information to some extents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Visualization</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We visualize the reasoning process of our multi-video collaboration framework in Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.3 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. The query is “<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What was the exact function of the machine used by the man?</span>”, which requires high-level and domain-related knowledge and the baseline model failed to provide a satisfying response. In our framework, the video is represented as graph-structured data, maintaining the key spatio-temporal information. Then, through the cross-graph attention shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.3 Ablation study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning (early version)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, important sub-graph structures from related videos are fused into current graph features, supporting our model to provide an accurate answer.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces a pioneering framework that enhances video large language models through structured multi-video collaborative reasoning. We first design the Video Structuring Module that models video as a spatio-temporal graph. Then, The Graph Fusion Module integrates related video information into enhanced graph tokens, which are then combined with visual and textual tokens in a multi-video structured prompt for input to the language model.
Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness and robustness of our approach to comprehending complex video content and answering queries accurately. We hope our work can provide insights for reliable video understanding and stimulate more research interest.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
